          "<strong>Legacy Record Attrition and the Consistency Mandate:</strong> Under <strong>26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(5)</strong> and 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d), a taxpayer claiming the RRC must substantiate both the numerator (QREs) and denominator (gross receipts) of its Fixed-Base Percentage across the applicable historical window (1984–1988 for traditional firms, or Years 4 through 10 of post-1993 QRE activity for start-up firms) and apply a consistent definition of QREs across base and credit years. In judicial decisions such as <em>Research, Inc. v. United States</em>, 95 AFTR 2d 2005-2483 (D. Minn. 2005), and <em>Trinity Industries, Inc. v. United States</em>, 691 F. Supp. 2d 688 (N.D. Tex. 2010), aff'd in part, 757 F.3d 420 (5th Cir. 2014), courts have strictly enforced the requirement that taxpayers maintain verifiable documentation supporting base-period numbers and consistency adjustments. Missing 1984–1988 ledgers or unsubstantiated historical ratios expose RRC claims to <a href=\"/us/faq/what-happens-if-rd-tax-credit-is-denied\">complete disallowance</a> upon IRS examination.",
          "<strong>Mergers, Acquisitions, and Dispositions (IRC § 41(f)(3)):</strong> Under <strong><a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41f-rd-tax-credit-special-rules\">26 U.S.C. § 41(f)(3)(A)</a></strong> (acquisitions) and <strong>§ 41(f)(3)(B)</strong> (dispositions), whenever a taxpayer acquires or disposes of the major portion of a trade or business (or the major portion of a separate unit of a trade or business), both the historical QREs and the historical gross receipts of the acquired or disposed business must be folded into or stripped out of the taxpayer's base-period computation across all relevant historical measurement years. For acquisitive corporations, reconstructing the 1984–1988 or start-up base-period QREs of multiple acquired target companies is frequently impossible, rendering the RRC legally indefensible."
