        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "For domestic R&E expenditures paid or incurred in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2021, and before January 1, 2025, that were previously capitalized and partially amortized under TCJA § 174, Congress enacted two distinct transition pathways in OBBBA § 70302(f), which are operationally governed by IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-28. First, any taxpayer may elect an accounting method change to recover remaining unamortized domestic R&E balances either entirely in its first taxable year beginning after December 31, 2024 (the 2025 calendar tax year), or ratably over two taxable years (2025 and 2026). Second, eligible small business taxpayers—defined as businesses meeting the I.R.C. § 448(c) gross receipts test and not classified as a tax shelter—may elect on or before July 6, 2026, to apply I.R.C. § 174A retroactively to taxable years 2022 through 2024 via <a href=\"/us/glossary/amended-tax-return-rd-credit\">amended returns</a> or Administrative Adjustment Requests (AARs). Finally, to prevent a double tax benefit from claiming both a full deduction and a full tax credit on the same dollar of expenditure, amended <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-280cc-rd-credit-deduction-disallowance\">I.R.C. § 280C(c)(1)</a> mandates that any domestic R&E deduction or capital account charge under I.R.C. § 174A must be reduced by the full amount of the I.R.C. § 41(a) research credit claimed, unless the taxpayer makes a timely protective election under I.R.C. § 280C(c)(2) on Form 6765 to claim a reduced research credit."
