          "<strong>IRS documentation focus.</strong> For <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-guidance-rd-tax-credit-refund-claims\">research credit refund claims</a> postmarked on or after January 10, 2022, the IRS requires five items of information: the business components to which the claim relates; for each business component, the research activities performed; the individuals who performed each activity; the information each individual sought to discover; and the total qualified employee wage, supply and <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b3-contract-research-expenses\">contract research expenses</a> for the claim year. <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-6765\">Form 6765</a> may be used for the expense totals. In June 2024 the IRS modified these requirements, so that refund claims postmarked after June 18, 2024 must identify all business components and their related research activities and provide the expense totals, while the names of individuals and the information each sought are no longer required at the time of filing. The five items apply to formal and informal claims, and Form 1120 filers must use a formal amended return. During a transition period, a deficient claim is notified by letter and the taxpayer has 45 days to perfect it before the IRS makes a final determination. <strong>Update:</strong> the IRS has extended that transition period several times; on October 1, 2025 (IR-2025-99) it extended it through January 10, 2027.",
