      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "11. Conclusion"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Customizing a CRM can generate qualifying research under I.R.C. §41, but only for the specific business components, or subsets of components under the shrinking-back rule, where the team faced a real technological uncertainty about capability, method or appropriate design and resolved it through a documented process of experimentation that relied on computer science or engineering principles. That work must meet the 80 percent substantially-all requirement for each business component and must avoid the statutory exclusions for cosmetic changes, post-production activity, adaptation to a particular customer's needs, duplication, and business-process studies. Back-office CRM functionality classed as internal use software must additionally be innovative, involve significant economic risk, and not be commercially available, a demanding combination for work built on a COTS platform. CRM functionality that lets customers or other third parties interact with the taxpayer's system is not internal use software, and dual function CRM software may qualify through a third-party subset or the 25 percent safe harbor. Development costs for qualifying CRM software are research or experimental expenditures under §174A for tax years beginning after December 31, 2024. Finally, the case law and IRS procedure reviewed show that U.S. claims succeed or fail on evidence: generalized assertions of uncertainty and reconstructed estimates of experimentation time have not been enough, refund claims must contain the five items of information the IRS requires, and, for tax years beginning after 2025, Form 6765 Section G requires business-component-level reporting."
      }
