      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Statutory Exclusions Under IRC Section 41(d)(4)"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Even where technical work involves engineering or computer science personnel, <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d4-rd-tax-credit-excluded-activities\">IRC Section 41(d)(4)</a>, Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c), and the Form 6765 instructions strictly exclude eight specific categories of activity from qualifying as research. First, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(A) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(2), any activity conducted <a href=\"/us/glossary/research-after-commercial-production\">after the beginning of commercial production</a> of the business component—when the component is ready for commercial sale or use or meets the taxpayer's basic functional and economic requirements—is statutorily ineligible, including preproduction planning, tooling-up for production, trial production runs, and troubleshooting faults in production equipment. Second, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(B) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(3), activities aimed at <a href=\"/us/glossary/adaptation-exclusion\">adapting an existing business component</a> to a particular customer's requirement or need are excluded. Third, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(C) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(4), the statute bars the <a href=\"/us/glossary/duplication-exclusion\">duplication</a> or reverse engineering of an existing business component (in whole or in part) from a physical examination of the component itself or from plans, blueprints, detailed specifications, or publicly available information."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Fourth, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(D) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(5), routine business operations—including efficiency surveys, activities relating to management function or technique, market research, testing, or development (including advertising or promotions), <a href=\"/us/glossary/quality-control-testing\">routine quality control testing</a> or inspection, and routine data collection—are excluded. Fifth, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(F) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(7), any <a href=\"/us/glossary/foreign-research-exclusion\">research conducted outside the United States</a>, the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, or any territory or possession of the United States is ineligible for the credit, requiring apportionment when services are performed both inside and outside the United States. Sixth, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(G) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(8), research in the <a href=\"/us/glossary/social-sciences-exclusion\">social sciences</a>, arts, or humanities is excluded."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Seventh, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(E) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(6), with respect to computer software developed by or for the benefit of the taxpayer primarily for internal use in general and administrative (G&A) functions (financial management, human resource management, and support services)—which must be categorized in Section G, Column 49(e) of the revised Form 6765—costs are excluded unless the software is developed to be commercially sold, leased, licensed, or marketed to third parties or to interact with third parties (Non-IUS, Option C), is used in an activity that constitutes qualified research or in a production process that meets the Four-Part Test (Excepted from IUS Treatment, Option D), or satisfies the rigorous three-part High Threshold of Innovation (HTI) test under Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii) (applicable to IUS, Option A, and the 25% safe harbor for <a href=\"/us/glossary/dual-function-software\">Dual Function Software</a> [DFS], Option B). To pass the <a href=\"/us/glossary/high-threshold-of-innovation-test\">HTI test</a>, the taxpayer must establish that: (1) the software is innovative, resulting in a substantial and economically significant reduction in cost, improvement in speed, or other measurable improvement; (2) the software development involves significant economic risk, where the taxpayer commits substantial resources and there is substantial uncertainty, because of technical risk, that those resources would be recovered within a reasonable period; and (3) the software is not commercially available for use by the taxpayer without modifications that themselves satisfy the first two HTI requirements. Eighth, under IRC Section 41(d)(4)(H) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(c)(9), any research to the extent <a href=\"/us/glossary/funded-research\">funded by any grant, contract, or otherwise by another person</a> or governmental entity is excluded whenever the taxpayer performing the research does not retain substantial rights in the research results or does not bear the financial risk of development failure."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "IRS Examination Risk Triggers, Notice 2002-44, and Substantiation Architecture"
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Automated Screening and Comparative QRE Analysis"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<a href=\"/us/faq/what-triggers-irs-rd-tax-credit-audit\">Examination selection</a> for Form 6765 claims combines automated statistical screening with targeted examiner review."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under the <em><a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-audit-techniques-guide-atg\">IRS Audit Techniques Guide (ATG)</a>: Credit for Increasing Research Activities (IRC § 41)</em> (Determining the Scope), examiners begin their risk assessment by executing a multi-year comparative spreadsheet analysis of QREs reported on Form 6765, evaluating wages, supplies, computer leasing, and contract research expenses against prior and subsequent tax years in the context of the taxpayer's overall business operations. Unexplained spikes in total QREs on Line 48, shifts in the ratio of supplies (Line 43) or contractor costs (Line 45) to wages (Line 42), high officer wage disclosures on Section E (Line 38), unadjusted M&A events on Line 39, or newly added QRE expense categories on Line 40 immediately flag the return for in-depth examination."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "The \"Prepackaged Submission\" Trap and Contemporaneous Nexus"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The most pervasive cause of credit disallowance during an IRS examination is the failure to establish <a href=\"/us/glossary/project-to-expense-nexus\">nexus</a>—the verifiable accounting and technical bridge connecting specific QRE dollars (employee hours, consumed prototype supplies, leased developmental computers, and contractor invoices) to specific experimental activities performed on an identified business component. Both the general <em>IRS Audit Techniques Guide (IRC § 41)</em> and the <em>Research Credit Claims Audit Techniques Guide (RCCATG)</em> explicitly warn examiners against relying on generic, retrospective \"prepackaged submissions\" (often delivered in multi-binder studies prepared by third-party consultants) that emphasize general estimation methodologies while failing to substantiate with competent evidence that the taxpayer actually paid or incurred QREs for qualified activities."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Crucially, the IRS ATGs note that audit adjustments grounded solely on an examiner's critique of a taxpayer's general estimation methodology—without developing the underlying factual record—are difficult for the IRS to sustain in Appeals or litigation. Consequently, IRS examiners are instructed to bypass boilerplate consultant narratives, reject unsupported departmental cost-center estimates or non-statistical judgment samples, and issue IDRs or summonses demanding primary source records. Under the ATG, routine IDR requests include the general ledger chart of accounts, QRE accounting procedures, reconciliation workpapers bridging the general ledger to Form 6765, all back-up workpapers and risk/exposure analyses retained by the third-party study provider, contemporaneous project test logs, and—where employee surveys, questionnaires, or interviews were used—copies of the survey instruments, instructions, timestamps, and underlying responses. Furthermore, <a href=\"/us/glossary/credible-oral-testimony\">oral testimony</a> or retrospective interviews conducted months or years after the tax year without corroborating contemporaneous documentation (as required under Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-4(d) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.6001-1, enforceable via a Notice of Inadequate Records under Treasury Regulation Section 1.6001-1(d)) are routinely rejected under judicial precedents such as <em>Eustace v. Commissioner</em>, T.C. Memo. 2001-66, aff'd, 312 F.3d 905 (7th Cir. 2002)."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Procedural Completeness and Notice 2002-44 / Refund Claim Specificity"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Procedural defects on Form 6765 create immediate administrative exposure even when underlying R&D activities are technically sound. The IRS ATG instructs examiners that if Form 6765 is not properly completed in accordance with its instructions—which includes completing Header Items A and B, mandatory Section E, and mandatory Section F in 2024, and Section G once mandatory—the examiner must require the taxpayer to cure the deficiencies and provide the relevant information before accepting or examining the claim."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "For <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-guidance-rd-tax-credit-refund-claims\">claims for credit or refund on amended returns</a>, the administrative rules evolved through three distinct stages that must be precisely distinguished. First, under Notice 2002-44, 2002-2 C.B. 39 (cited in the 2005 <em>IRS Audit Techniques Guide</em>), corporate Form 1120 research credit refund claims were directed to the Ogden Service Center (with a separate procedure for certain taxpayers under audit to submit claims to the LMSB/SBSE manager) and were required to include a completed Form 6765, set forth in detail each ground upon which the credit was claimed, provide facts sufficient to apprise the IRS of the exact basis of the claim under Treasury Regulation Section 301.6402-2(b)(1), and be verified under penalties of perjury. Second, in Notice 2008-39, 2008-13 I.R.B. 684 (reflected in the <em>RCCATG</em> and Form 6765 instructions), the IRS explicitly superseded Notice 2002-44, eliminating the procedure allowing taxpayers under examination to submit research credit claims directly to IRS auditors and requiring paper corporate refund claims to be filed on a formal amended return at the Ogden Service Center labeled \"Refund-Research Credit\" with both the original Form 6765 (if any) and an amended Form 6765 attached."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Third, under <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-ccm-20214101f-impact-rd-claims\">IRS Chief Counsel Memorandum (CCM) 20214101F</a> (effective January 10, 2022) as streamlined by the IRS on June 18, 2024, and integrated into the Instructions for Form 6765 (Rev. January 2025, \"Research Credit Claims on Amended Returns\"), a taxpayer filing an amended return (or administrative adjustment request [AAR]) claiming a new or increased Section 41 credit must provide upfront: (1) identification of all business components to which the Section 41 claim relates for that tax year; (2) for each business component, identification of all research activities performed and—by completing <strong>Section G, Column 49(f)</strong> of the revised Form 6765—a description of <strong>\"the information sought to be discovered\"</strong>; and (3) the total qualified employee wage expenses, supply expenses, computer rental/lease expenses, and contract research expenses for the claim year (which may be satisfied using Form 6765). In its June 18, 2024 FAQ update, the IRS waived the upfront requirement to list the specific names or job titles of the individuals who performed each research activity (former Item 3) and the information each individual sought to discover (former Item 4) on the initial amended return submission, reserving employee-specific requests for substantive examination. During the IRS transition period (extended through January 10, 2027), taxpayers notified of a deficient refund claim via Letter 6426C or Letter 6428 are granted 45 days to perfect the claim via paper mail or fax (portable electronic storage devices are not accepted), after which unperfected claims are rejected without Appeals rights."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Strategic Compliance Synthesis for 2024 and 2025 Filers"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The 2024 Form 6765 instructions mark a decisive transition in federal research credit administration. Even though Section G remains optional for 2024 original tax returns (and through 2025 under the extended transition period prior to mandatory enforcement for tax years beginning after 2025), the mandatory implementation of Header Items A and B, Section E (Lines 37–41), and Section F (Lines 42–48) in 2024—followed by the mandatory enforcement of Section G's 80%/Top 50 business component reporting—requires taxpayers to treat the 2024–2025 filing cycles as an operational dry run."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "To ensure full compliance with the revised Form 6765 instructions and insulate claims from IRS examination adjustments, taxpayers and tax professionals must execute four structural actions across their accounting and technical workflows. First, organizations must reconfigure cost accounting around discrete business components so that general ledger chart-of-account structures and <a href=\"/us/faq/best-software-tracking-rd-time-expenses\">engineering time-tracking systems</a> map directly to individual project identifiers (Column 49(c)), component types (Column 49(d)), and software classifications (Column 49(e)) rather than broad departmental cost centers, separating wages into direct conduct (Column 50), direct supervision (Column 51), and direct support (Column 52) while isolating corporate officers' wages on Section E, Line 38. Second, claimants must enforce <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">contemporaneous technical substantiation</a> by time-stamping technical unknowns and iterative hypothesis-testing logs during the tax year as experimentation occurs, establishing an auditable nexus between each business component's technical Four-Part Test narrative and its Section G expenditure columns (and completing Column 49(f) for any amended return refund claim). Third, before selecting Section A (Regular Credit) or Section B (ASC), claimants must audit base-period consistency under IRC Section 41(c)(5) and Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-3(d) (or 3-year lookback consistency under Treasury Regulation Section 1.41-9(c)(2)), apply the June 18, 2025 IRS clarification for separate-filing controlled group members in Sections E and F and on Lines 5 and 20, and verify that officers' wages (Line 38), business acquisitions or dispositions (Line 39), new QRE categories (Line 40), ASC 730 Directive amounts (Line 41), and total business component counts (Line 37) in Section E reconcile precisely with underlying workpapers. Finally, because the IRC Section 280C(c) reduced credit election—made on Header Item A on Page 1 and calculated on Line 13 (at 15.8%) or Line 26 (at 79%)—is strictly irrevocable and can only be made on an original timely filed return (including extensions), taxpayers must model the net tax impact of Section 280C(c) alongside IRC Section 174 (and post-2024 IRC Section 174A) capitalization and <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-174-amortization\">amortization schedules</a> prior to filing the original 2024 return."
      }
