        "text": "To illustrate the exact financial mechanics of a disallowed R&D claim, consider the baseline liability model in which a corporation claims an R&D tax credit or offset valued at $100,000. If an IRS or ATO audit rejects the claim in full due to insufficient substantiation, the taxpayer must first repay (or forfeit) the entire $100,000 principal credit to the revenue authority. Under the U.S. Negligence or Erroneous Refund (IRC § 6662 / IRC § 6676) 20%), resulting in a total cash loss of $120,000 before compounding interest. Under the Australian Lack of Reasonable Care (25%), bringing the total obligation to $125,000 prior to shortfall interest charges. If the infraction rises to a U.S. Gross Valuation Misstatement (IRC § 6662(h)) at the 40%) elevates the total outlay to $140,000, while an Australian finding of Recklessness (50%) for a combined liability of $150,000. Finally, a determination of U.S. Civil Fraud (IRC § 6663(a)) at the 75%) to the $100,000 principal clawback, generating a $175,000 balance-sheet loss prior to daily compounding interest. Because multi-year R&D examinations frequently span 24 to 48 months from the original return filing date, daily compounding interest under IRC § 6601 and IRC § 6622 applied simultaneously to both the $100,000 deficiency and the statutory penalty routinely adds another 15% to 35% to the combined obligation."
