          "<strong>Multi-year lock-in contracts are not standard among premier specialist R&D tax advisors:</strong> Binding a corporate taxpayer for three to five years shifts advisory performance risk onto the client in a high-scrutiny IRS enforcement environment where the taxpayer bears 100% of the statutory liability for disallowed credits and <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-penalties-for-improper-rd-tax-credit-claim\">IRC Section 6662/6676 penalties</a>.",
          "<strong>Organic retention through transparent risk-sharing eliminates principal-agent conflicts.</strong>",
          "<strong>Multi-year tax complexity requires technical continuity earned annually, not legal coercion:</strong> While federal rules under IRC Sections 41, 174, 174A, and <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-280cc-rd-credit-deduction-disallowance\">280C(c)</a> and state-level regimes (such as Maine's 15-year credit carryforward under 36 M.R.S. § 5219-K, Maryland's automatic decoupling from OBBBA IRC Section 174A, Oklahoma's $20 million SB 324 rebate cap, Louisiana's 0.5% LED fee floor under La. R.S. 47:6015, and Arizona's tiered credit under A.R.S. § 43-1168) span 5-to-15-year planning horizons, a specialist R&D tax advisor secures long-term client relationships by absorbing first-year switching friction and delivering audit-ready technical substantiation every single year."
