        "text": "Under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d2-business-component-test\">26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(2)(A)</a>, the Four-Part Test must be applied separately to each business component—defined in § 41(d)(2)(B) as any product, process, computer software, technique, formula, or invention to be held for sale, lease, or license, or used by the taxpayer in its trade or business. Importantly for breweries and distilleries, <strong>26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(2)(C)</strong> establishes a Special Rule for Production Processes: any plant process, machinery, or technique for the commercial production of a beverage is treated as a separate business component distinct from the beverage being produced. Thus, even if a brewery is producing an established flagship lager, an experimental project to redesign the wort cooling loop, automate fermentation temperature control, or reduce canning line DO pickup is evaluated as its own standalone process business component."
