        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Fourth, Contract Research Expenses (<a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b3-contract-research-expenses\">26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(3)(A)</a>; 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)) are generally limited to 65% of amounts paid to third-party non-employees (such as external microbiology laboratories conducting HS-GC-FID-FPD or ELISA gluten assays, external fermentation consultants, or packaging engineering firms; rising to 75% for qualified research consortia under § 41(b)(3)(C) and 100% for eligible small businesses, universities, or federal laboratories performing qualified energy research under § 41(b)(3)(D)). Under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)(2)–(3), contract research qualifies only if a three-part test is met: (1) the agreement is entered into prior to performance of the research, (2) the research is performed <strong>on behalf of the taxpayer</strong> (the taxpayer retains <a href=\"/us/glossary/substantial-rights\">substantial rights</a> to the results, even if non-exclusive), and (3) the taxpayer bears the economic risk of failure (must pay even if the research fails; success-contingent fees are disqualified). Under § 41(b)(3)(B), prepaid contract research is creditable only in the tax year the research is actually conducted."
