          "<strong>Step 3 — <a href=\"/us/faq/how-to-calculate-rd-tax-credit-2024\">Federal R&D Tax Credit Calculation</a> on Form 6765 (ASC Method and IRC § 280C Election):</strong> Taxpayers compute their federal credit on Form 6765 using either the <strong>Regular Credit (Section A)</strong>—which equals <strong>20%</strong> of current-year QREs exceeding a base amount (with a start-up fixed-base percentage of 3% for the first 5 years and a statutory maximum fixed-base percentage of 16% under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41c3-fixed-base-percentage\">26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(3)(C)</a>)—or the <strong>Alternative Simplified Credit (ASC, Section B)</strong>. If the hypothetical brewery is in its fourth year of claims and beyond with an established prior 3-year average QRE of <strong>$80,000</strong>, the <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41c4-alternative-simplified-credit\">ASC base amount</a> is 50% of that 3-year average ($80,000 x 0.50 = <strong>$40,000</strong>), and the gross credit is <strong>14%</strong> of current-year QREs exceeding $40,000: the Generalist claim yields ($50,000 - $40,000) x 0.14 = <strong>$1,400</strong>, whereas the Specialist claim yields ($110,000 - $40,000) x 0.14 = <strong>$9,800</strong> (a <strong>7.00x</strong> increase in gross credit because the historical base amount is fixed). Finally, under <strong><a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-280cc-rd-credit-deduction-disallowance\">26 U.S.C. § 280C(c)</a></strong> and Form 6765 Line 26 instructions, a taxpayer making the irrevocable § 280C reduced-credit election on its original timely filed return multiplies the gross credit by <strong>79%</strong> (100% minus the 21% corporate tax rate) to retain 100% of its immediate domestic § 174A deduction, resulting in a net federal tax credit of <strong>$7,742.00</strong> ($9,800 x 0.79) under the Specialist claim compared to <strong>$1,106.00</strong> ($1,400 x 0.79) under the Generalist claim."
