        "text": "To quantify baseline audit failure rates across the general claimant population, the underlying analysis utilizes compliance examination data from the United Kingdom's tax authority—referred to in legacy source literature as Her Majesty's Revenue and Customs (HMRC), and officially designated His Majesty's Revenue and Customs since September 2022—specifically drawing upon findings from HMRC's Mandatory Random Enquiry Programme (MREP) sample as an empirical proxy for systemic compliance defects across self-assessed R&D tax regimes. According to the cited MREP compliance data, approximately 25% of randomly examined R&D claims were disallowed in their entirety because examining officers determined that no qualifying R&D activity took place under the governing statutory definition. Furthermore, an additional 19% of examined claims involved overclaimed relief, representing instances where bona fide qualifying research activity occurred, but the claimant miscalculated or improperly apportioned qualifying expenditures, thereby necessitating material downward adjustment. Aggregating these two failure modes establishes a combined 44% rate of substantial non-compliance across the general market sample (25% + 19% = 44%), leaving only 56% of randomly audited claims unadjusted following examination."
