        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The second-order analytical implication of reconciling these two datasets is that corporate audit exposure must be evaluated across two distinct statistical dimensions: the probability of an audit adjustment occurring (the incidence rate) and the fractional dollar severity of the downward haircut when an adjustment is imposed (the loss-given-adjustment magnitude). In the general market, a 35% to 44% incidence of non-compliance combines with a high severity of disallowance—where over half of non-compliant claims (25% out of 44%, or 56.8% of adjusted claims) suffer a 100% total disallowance due to fundamental technical ineligibility—to produce an expected portfolio-wide value erosion of approximately 20%. Conversely, under a specialist compliance architecture, technical ineligibility is virtually eliminated prior to filing, meaning that the residual 1% to 2% of claims experiencing examination adjustments involve only minor financial apportionment refinements rather than total project rejections, compressing the expected portfolio-wide value loss to 0.5%."
