        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In determining the amount of the research credit under IRC § 41, all members of the same controlled group of corporations, and all trades or businesses (whether or not incorporated) which are <a href=\"/us/glossary/common-control\">under common control</a>, are treated as a single taxpayer (IRC § 41(f)(1)(A)(i), (B)(i)). Consequently, the group’s base amount—including its FBP and four-year average gross receipts—is computed on an <a href=\"/us/glossary/aggregation-rules\">aggregate basis</a> by combining the QREs and gross receipts of all group members after eliminating intra-group transactions (Treas. Reg. § 1.41-6(b)), and the resulting group credit is allocated among the members in proportion to their respective current-year QREs (IRC § 41(f)(1)(A)(ii), (B)(ii)). When a major portion of a trade or business (or a separate unit thereof) is acquired or disposed of after the beginning of the measurement period, the statute requires symmetrical adjustments: the QREs and gross receipts of the acquiring taxpayer for all prior periods taken into account under IRC § 41 are increased by the amount of QREs and gross receipts attributable to the acquired portion of the business, and the QREs and gross receipts of the disposing predecessor are correspondingly decreased, provided the disposing taxpayer furnishes the necessary historical information to the acquirer (<a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41f-rd-tax-credit-special-rules\">IRC § 41(f)(3)(A), (B)</a>). The IRS Audit Techniques Guide notes that the IRC § 41(f)(5) control test uses a \"more than 50 percent\" ownership threshold rather than the \"at least 80 percent\" threshold used for consolidated return purposes under IRC § 1563(a), making the <a href=\"/us/glossary/controlled-group\">§ 41 controlled group</a> broader than a consolidated tax return group."
