        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The <a href=\"/us/faq/asc-vs-regular-rd-tax-credit-method\">Alternative Simplified Credit</a> (ASC) uses a fundamentally different base calculation. At the election of the taxpayer, the credit determined under IRC § 41(a)(1) equals 14 percent of so much of the QREs for the taxable year as exceeds 50 percent of the average QREs for the three taxable years preceding the taxable year for which the credit is being determined (<a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41c4-alternative-simplified-credit\">IRC § 41(c)(4)(A)</a>). Thus, the effective \"base amount\" under the ASC is simply 50 percent of the rolling three-year average of prior QREs. If the taxpayer has no QREs in any one of those three preceding taxable years, the credit rate is reduced to 6 percent of current-year QREs with no base subtraction (IRC § 41(c)(4)(B); Treas. Reg. § 1.41-9(c)(1)). Just as under the regular credit, the QREs taken into account for the three preceding taxable years must be determined on a basis consistent with the determination of QREs for the credit year (Treas. Reg. § 1.41-9(c)(2)). Furthermore, if the credit year or any of the three preceding taxable years is a short taxable year, the QREs for the short taxable year are modified by annualizing or prorating based on the number of days in the short taxable year divided by 365 (Treas. Reg. § 1.41-9(c)(3)). Because the ASC base relies exclusively on the prior three years of QREs, it requires no 1984–1988 historical data and no gross receipts calculations."
