        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under U.S. federal tax law (26 U.S.C. § 41), supporting and surrounding activities in a biotechnology startup are governed instead by three U.S. statutory, regulatory, and judicial standards. First, under the 80% \"Substantially All\" Process-of-Experimentation Rule (26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1)(C) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(6)), rather than bifurcating projects into Australian \"core\" and \"supporting\" activities, U.S. Treasury Regulation 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(6) provides that if 80% or more of a taxpayer's research activities with respect to a business component (measured on a cost or other consistently applied reasonable basis) constitute elements of a process of experimentation for a qualified purpose, the \"substantially all\" requirement is satisfied even if the remaining 20% (or less) of research activities with respect to that business component do not constitute elements of a process of experimentation—provided those remaining activities satisfy the § 41(d)(1)(A) (§ 174A) test and are not excluded under § 41(d)(4). Second, under the Direct Support of Qualified Services Rule for Employee Wages (26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(B)(ii) and 26 CFR § 1.41-2(c)(3)), for employee wage QREs, \"direct support\" is strictly defined as services in the direct support of either persons engaging in the actual conduct of qualified research or first-line supervisors of such research (such as cleaning research equipment, compiling research data, or typing laboratory reports), excluding general administrative services."
