        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Preparing and defending an IRC § 41 research credit claim for a clinical-stage biotechnology startup requires bridging the divide between financial accounting ledgers and biomedical experimentation. Standard accounting records (general ledgers, W-2 payroll registers, and CRO invoices) demonstrate that costs were incurred, but cannot by themselves prove that the underlying activity sought to resolve a technical uncertainty regarding capability, method, or appropriate design (26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(3)(i)), relied on biological or physical sciences (§ 1.41-4(a)(4)), satisfied the 80% process-of-experimentation threshold (§ 1.41-4(a)(6)), or excluded High-Risk regulatory and post-commercial tasks under IRS Publication 5931."
