          "<strong><a href=\"/us/faq/section-41-credit-vs-section-174-expenses\">Independence from the Section 41 research credit</a>.</strong> Mandatory capitalization under Section 174 applies to all SRE expenditures regardless of whether the taxpayer claims or qualifies for the <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41a-rd-tax-credit-general-rule\">research credit under Section 41</a>. Notice 2023-63, Section 1, expressly confirms that the guidance governing Section 174 does not alter the rules for determining eligibility for, or calculating the amount of, the Section 41 credit. Because Section 41 <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b1-qualified-research-expenses\">Qualified Research Expenses (QREs)</a> are limited to direct in-house wages, direct supplies, computer rental costs, and 65% (or 75%/100% for certain consortia and eligible contractors) of contract research expenses, a taxpayer's Section 174 SRE expenditure base (which includes facilities overhead, depreciation, patent legal fees, and 100% of qualifying contract research) is substantially broader than its Section 41 QRE base.",
