      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "5. Cost Segregation, Software Segmentation, and Specialized Compliance Architecture"
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "5.1 Structural Divergence Between Section 174/174A SREs and Section 41 QREs"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "<strong>Broader Section 174/174A Scope:</strong> Encompasses direct research wages, supplies, overhead and indirect costs incident to research (such as utilities, laboratory/facility depreciation, rent, and patent prosecution and attorney fees under <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/26/1.174-2\">26 CFR § 1.174-2</a>), 100 percent of qualifying contract research where the taxpayer bears risk or holds exploitation rights, all software development costs under Section 174(c)(3), and foreign R&E (which must still be strictly segregated from domestic R&E and capitalized over 15 years under amended Section 174). Furthermore, state and local tax (SALT) non-conformity requires separate multi-state ledger tracking in jurisdictions that decouple from federal TCJA capitalization or fail to conform automatically to OBBBA Section 174A immediate expensing (<a href=\"https://perkinsaccounting.com/blog/tax-treatment-research-experimentation-software-development-2022-beyond/\">Perkins & Co</a>).",
          "<strong>Narrower Section 41 QRE Scope and the Four-Part Test:</strong> Restricted strictly to domestic research wages (excluding nontaxable fringe benefits), supplies consumed in research, computer rental or cloud hosting used directly in research, and 65 percent (or 75 percent / 100 percent for qualified research consortia and eligible energy research) of qualifying <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b3-contract-research-expenses\">contract research</a>, with zero overhead, indirect facility costs, or patent legal fees permitted. To qualify under <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/41\">IRC Section 41(d)</a>, each business component activity must satisfy the statutory <strong>four-part test</strong>:",
          "<strong>Section 174 Test:</strong> Expenditures must qualify as research or experimental expenditures eligible under Section 174 (or Section 174A).",
          "<strong>Technological in Nature Test:</strong> The activity must be undertaken for the purpose of discovering information that is <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d1-qualified-research-definition\">technological in nature</a>, fundamentally relying on the principles of the physical or biological sciences, engineering, or computer science.",
          "<strong><a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d3-qualified-research-permitted-purposes\">Permitted Purpose</a> / Elimination of Uncertainty Test:</strong> The application of that technological information must be intended to be useful in the development of a new or improved business component of the taxpayer (product, process, computer software, technique, formula, or invention), specifically seeking to eliminate technical uncertainty concerning the capability, method, or appropriate design for developing or improving the component's function, performance, reliability, or quality.",
          "<strong>Process of Experimentation Test:</strong> <a href=\"/us/glossary/substantially-all-rule\">Substantially all</a> (at least 80 percent under Treasury Regulations Section 1.41-4(a)(6)) of the activities must constitute elements of a process of experimentation involving the identification of uncertainty, evaluation of one or more alternatives, and systematic modeling, simulation, or trial-and-error testing."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "5.2 Internal Use Software (IUS) and the Dual-Function Safe Harbor"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "While all software development costs fall within Section 174(c)(3) for deduction or capitalization purposes, software developed primarily for internal use—defined under <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/26/1.41-4\">26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)</a> and Treasury Decision 9786 (<a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/irb/2016-42_IRB\">IRB 2016-42</a>) as software developed by (or for the benefit of) the taxpayer for use in general and administrative functions (specifically financial management, human resources management, and support services) that facilitate or support the conduct of the taxpayer's trade or business—is excluded from the Section 41 credit unless it satisfies an additional three-part <strong>high threshold of innovation test</strong>:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "ordered": true,
        "items": [
          "The software is <strong>innovative</strong> (intended to result in a reduction in cost or improvement in speed or other measurable operating improvement that is substantial and economically significant, if development is successful);",
          "The software development involves <strong><a href=\"/us/glossary/significant-economic-risk\">significant economic risk</a></strong> (the taxpayer commits substantial resources to the development and there is substantial uncertainty, because of technical risk, that such resources would be recovered within a reasonable period); and",
          "The software is <strong>not <a href=\"/us/glossary/commercial-availability-standard\">commercially available</a></strong> for use by the taxpayer (cannot be purchased, leased, or licensed and used for the intended purpose without modifications that would themselves satisfy the first two requirements)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Where software is presumed or determined to serve both general/administrative functions and third-party interactions (\"dual-function computer software\"), <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/26/1.41-4\">26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi)</a> provides an administrative safe harbor: after using reasonable criteria to isolate exclusively internal-use and exclusively third-party subsets, a taxpayer may include 25 percent of the QREs of the remaining dual-function subset in computing the Section 41 credit, provided that the third-party subset satisfies the three-part high threshold of innovation test and third-party use is reasonably estimated at the beginning of development to constitute at least 10 percent of the dual-function subset's total use."
      }
