        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<strong>Section 174A(c).</strong> Although Section 174A(a) restores immediate expensing as the default rule for domestic research and software development expenditures, taxpayers with net operating losses, international minimum tax exposure, or specific income-smoothing objectives may prefer to defer deductions. Under Section 174A(c)(1), at the election of the taxpayer, domestic research or experimental expenditures which would (but for Section 174A) be chargeable to capital account but are not chargeable to property of a character subject to depreciation under Section 167 or depletion under Section 611 may be treated as deferred expenses and amortized ratably over such period of not less than 60 months as may be selected by the taxpayer, beginning with the month in which the taxpayer first realizes benefits from such expenditures. Under Section 174A(c)(2), this election may be made for any taxable year, but must be made not later than the time prescribed by law for filing the return for such taxable year (including extensions thereof). Once elected, both the method of charging domestic expenditures to capital account and the amortization period selected by the taxpayer must be adhered to in computing taxable income for the election year and for all subsequent taxable years unless, with the approval of the Secretary of the Treasury, a change to a different method (or to a different period) is authorized with respect to part or all of such expenditures. The election under Section 174A(c) does not apply to any item paid or incurred during any taxable year before the taxable year for which the election is made."
