      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "7. Audit Scrutiny and Substantiation Standards"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "While the IRS possesses general statutory examination authority under IRC Section 7602 to examine books, records, and cost classifications for any open taxable year within the Section 6501 statute of limitations, <strong>no provision in the TCJA, Pub. L. 119-21, Notice 2023-63, or Rev. Proc. 2025-28 grants a special new statutory authorization to retroactively reclassify pre-2022 software development costs under post-2021 standards.</strong> On the contrary, section 1 of Notice 2023-63 explicitly states: <em>\"The guidance in this notice does not apply for purposes of determining whether an expenditure paid or incurred for taxable years beginning before January 1, 2022, is a research or experimental expenditure under § 174 as in effect for taxable years beginning before January 1, 2022 (former § 174).\"</em> Furthermore, section 7.01 of Rev. Proc. 2025-23 (and prior automatic accounting method change guidance in Rev. Proc. 2023-11) expressly provides that the cut-off change to TCJA Section 174 for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2021, does not provide audit protection for expenditures paid or incurred in taxable years beginning before January 1, 2022, while simultaneously confirming that pre-2022 years remain governed strictly by former Section 174 and Rev. Proc. 2000-50. For taxable years beginning in 2022 and thereafter, however, taxpayers claiming either the Section 41 R&D credit, the § 70302(f)(1) small business retroactive election, the § 70302(f)(2) unamortized recovery election, or immediate domestic expensing under Section 174A(a) vs. 15-year foreign amortization under Section 174(a) must maintain <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">contemporaneous project accounting records</a>, geographic labor tracking (to substantiate domestic versus foreign research locations under Notice 2023-63 section 3.04), and clear delineation between qualifying software development activities (section 5.03) and non-qualifying maintenance or configuration costs (sections 5.04–5.05)."
      }
