      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Contractual protection is a non-listed category, so for an R&D engagement whose only reportable feature is a contingent fee, the range is $5,000 to $50,000 (with the $5,000 floor and $10,000 ceiling applying to natural persons, and the $10,000 floor and $50,000 ceiling applying to other taxpayers). The $200,000 ceiling applies only to listed transactions."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Audit exposure behind the fee"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Aprio states that claiming the credit on a timely filed return does not automatically <a href=\"/us/faq/what-triggers-irs-rd-tax-credit-audit\">trigger an audit</a>, and that an unsuccessful examination can lead to full disallowance and additional penalties. Where a contingency fee is non-refundable, the taxpayer bears the full fee even if the credit is lost."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "That expectation is dated. Treasury Inspector General for Tax Administration report 2026-IE-R003 (issued March 25, 2026) states that, as of January 2026, Congress had cut IRA funding to $26 billion through rescissions totaling $53.5 billion, $41.8 billion of it from enforcement, leaving $3.8 billion for enforcement. A later TIGTA report (2026-3S0-045, issued August 26, 2026) states that the IRS exhausted that remaining $3.8 billion of IRA enforcement funding as of December 31, 2025, and projects fiscal year 2026 enforcement spending about 29% below fiscal year 2025."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under the <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-6765\">Form 6765</a> instructions (Rev. December 2025, released in final form in February 2026) and IR-2025-99 (October 1, 2025), Section G (Business Component Information) is optional for all filers for tax years beginning before 2026 and required for tax years beginning after 2025. Reporting remains optional for qualified small businesses electing the <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41h-payroll-tax-credit-qualified-small-businesses\">payroll tax credit</a>, and for taxpayers with total QREs of $1.5 million or less and gross receipts of $50 million or less who claim the credit on an original return. Separately, <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-guidance-rd-tax-credit-refund-claims\">research credit refund claims</a> postmarked after June 18, 2024, must identify each business component and its research activities and report total qualified wage, supply, and contract research expenses; the IRS has extended the 45-day perfection period for such claims through January 10, 2027. Because many contingent-fee engagements are built around <a href=\"/us/glossary/amended-tax-return-rd-credit\">amended-return refund claims</a>, these documentation requirements directly affect the work an advisor must perform to earn a percentage fee."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Analysis: does \"no benefit, no fee\" avoid the contingency rules?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "As analysis only: §10.27(c)(1) covers fees based \"in whole or in part\" on whether a position is sustained, and §1.6011-4(b)(4)(i) covers fees contingent on realizing tax benefits. A fee that is <a href=\"/us/faq/rd-tax-advisor-fees-if-no-credit-found\">waived when no benefit results</a> is at least partly contingent on the outcome. The proposed §10.51(b)(2) definition in REG-116610-20 would use similarly broad outcome-based language. Whether a given engagement letter falls within any of these rules depends on its terms; taxpayers should obtain advice on their specific arrangement."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Conclusion"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The most-cited \"standard\" contingency fee for R&D tax credit work is 25% to 40% of the credit (Source Advisors), with other providers citing 35% for boutiques and 20% to 30% for specialists. These are commentary figures, not regulated rates. On a credit of 6% to 8% of QREs, <a href=\"/us/faq/is-30-percent-rd-tax-credit-fee-too-high\">a 30% fee</a> absorbs about 1.8% to 2.4% of the research budget (0.06 × 0.30 = 1.8%; 0.08 × 0.30 = 2.4%). Circular 230 §10.27 still bars contingent fees for original-return preparation, but <em>Ridgely v. Lew</em> removed the IRS's ability to enforce it against ordinary pre-audit refund claims. Proposed regulations (REG-116610-20) would replace §10.27 with a rule treating contingent fees for preparing original or amended returns and pre-examination refund claims as disreputable conduct; they had not been finalized as of November 10, 2026. Because the research credit is not excluded by Rev. Proc. 2007-20, a contingent R&D fee may make the engagement a reportable transaction requiring Form 8886, with a §6707A penalty of $5,000 to $50,000 for failing to disclose a non-listed transaction."
      }
