          "<strong>Wages for qualified services (<a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b2-in-house-research-expenses\">IRC §41(b)(2)(A)(i)</a>):</strong> Wages (as defined in IRC §3401(a) for income tax withholding purposes, excluding compensation taken into account in determining the work opportunity credit under §51) paid or incurred to an employee for \"qualified services.\" Under IRC §41(b)(2)(B), <a href=\"/us/glossary/qualified-services\">qualified services</a> consist of: (i) engaging in qualified research, (ii) directly supervising qualified research, or (iii) directly supporting qualified research. Under the \"<a href=\"/us/glossary/substantially-all-rule\">substantially all</a>\" rule of IRC §41(b)(2)(B) and Treas. Reg. §1.41-2(d)(2), if at least 80% of an employee's time during the tax year is spent performing qualified services, 100% of that employee's wages qualify as QREs. Under Treas. Reg. §1.41-2(c)(2), \"<a href=\"/us/glossary/direct-supervision\">direct supervision</a>\" means immediate, first-line supervision (such as a research lab group leader who directly reviews and guides experimental work); higher-level managers and executives to whom first-line supervisors report do not qualify, even if those executives are qualified scientists or engineers.",
          "<strong>Supplies used in qualified research (IRC §41(b)(2)(A)(ii)):</strong> Any amount paid or incurred for supplies used in the conduct of qualified research. Under IRC §41(b)(2)(C) and Treas. Reg. §1.41-2(b), \"supplies\" means any tangible property other than: (i) land or improvements to land, and (ii) property of a character subject to the allowance for depreciation. Examples include raw materials, electronic components, and laboratory chemicals consumed in fabricating and testing <a href=\"/us/glossary/prototype-development\">experimental prototypes</a>.",
          "<strong>Right to use computers in qualified research (IRC §41(b)(2)(A)(iii)):</strong> Under the statutory heading \"Right to use computers,\" in-house research expenses include any amount paid or incurred to another person for the right to use time-sharing or leased computers in the conduct of qualified research. Because supplies under §41(b)(2)(C) must be <em>tangible</em> property, <a href=\"/us/faq/aws-cloud-hosting-costs-rd-tax-credit\">hosted cloud computing payments</a> (such as AWS, Microsoft Azure, or Google Cloud compute instances dedicated to staging, simulation, and development environments, as opposed to commercial production web hosting) are properly classified under the separate statutory category of computer rental/lease expenses in IRC §41(b)(2)(A)(iii) and Treas. Reg. §1.41-2(b)(4).",
