          "<strong>The 2022–2024 TCJA Capitalization Period (<a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-174-research-expenditures-amortization\">IRC § 174</a>):</strong> Under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 (TCJA, P.L. 115-97), for tax years beginning after December 31, 2021, through December 31, 2024, taxpayers were required to capitalize and amortize <a href=\"/us/glossary/specified-research-or-experimental-expenditures-sre\">Specified Research or Experimental (SRE) expenditures</a>—which explicitly include all software development costs under IRC § 174(c)(3)—over 5 years for domestic research (using a mid-year convention that permitted only a 10% deduction in Year 1) and over 15 years (180 months) for foreign research. During that period, pairing <a href=\"/us/faq/section-41-credit-vs-section-174-expenses\">IRC § 174 compliance with IRC § 41 credit claiming</a> was essential to offset the severe cash-tax impact of deferred expense deductions.",
          "<strong>Post-2024 Domestic Expensing Restoration (IRC § 174A):</strong> Enacted on July 4, 2025, under the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (OBBBA, P.L. 119-21) and implemented via IRS procedural guidance in Rev. Proc. 2025-23, Rev. Proc. 2025-28, and Rev. Proc. 2026-32, Congress added <strong>IRC § 174A</strong>, which restores immediate 100% current-year deductibility for <strong>domestic</strong> research and experimental expenditures (including domestic software development costs) for tax years beginning after December 31, 2024 (with an optional election to amortize over at least 60 months). The legislation also established transition rules permitting studios to accelerate and deduct remaining <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-174-amortization\">unamortized 2022–2024 domestic SRE balances</a> in full in the first tax year beginning after December 31, 2024, or ratably over a two-year period (2025 and 2026), alongside a retroactive amended-return option for eligible small businesses (average annual gross receipts of $31 million or less) prior to July 6, 2026. Crucially, however, <strong>foreign</strong> research and experimental expenditures (such as payments to offshore development studios or foreign engineering subsidiaries outside the United States) remain governed by IRC § 174 and continue to require mandatory 15-year capitalization and amortization, nor do <a href=\"/us/glossary/foreign-research-exclusion\">foreign research activities</a> qualify for the federal IRC § 41 credit due to the geographic exclusion in IRC § 41(d)(4)(F)."
