        "text": "Under IRM 4.46.3.7.6 and directive LB&I-04-0125-0001, research credit refund claims are screened for these three procedural sufficiency items at IRS campus intake or by Area Planning and Special Programs (PSP) and field examiners within 30 days of receipt. If a timely filed claim under IRC § 6511(a) is determined to be deficient during the IRS transition period—which began January 10, 2022, was extended through January 10, 2026 in LB&I-04-0125-0001, and was subsequently extended twice for an additional four years through January 10, 2027 per official IRS FAQ guidance updated October 1, 2026—the IRS notifies the taxpayer via <strong>Letter 6428</strong> (<em>Claim for Credit for Increasing Research Credit Activities - Additional Information Required</em>) or <strong>Letter 6426C</strong>, detailing which required items are missing and granting the taxpayer <strong>45 days</strong> from the letter date to perfect the claim (accompanied by a new signed declaration under penalties of perjury). Only if the taxpayer fails to submit sufficient information within the 45-day perfection window (or files a deficient claim after the transition period expires) does the IRS reject processing of the claim by issuing <strong>Letter 6430</strong> (<em>No Consideration, Section 41 Claim</em>), which informs the taxpayer that the deficient claim cannot be considered (an administrative refusal to process rather than a formal substantive claim disallowance). Crucially, under LB&I-04-0125-0001 and IRM 4.46.3.7.6, prior to issuing Letter 6430 to any taxpayer, both <strong>IRS Management</strong> (specifically the Territory Manager for LB&I cases or the Area PSP Section Chief for Small Business/Self-Employed [SB/SE] cases) and <strong>IRS Office of Chief Counsel</strong> must formally review and concur that the claim fails to include the three required items. Thus, intake rejection is neither fully automated nor devoid of human and legal oversight."
