        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Unlike the federal reduced credit election—which applies a flat 21% reduction (79% multiplier) to all taxpayers based on the top corporate tax rate under IRC Section 11(b)—California R&TC Section 24440(b)(3) (together with R&TC Sections 17052.12 and 23609 and Form FTB 3523) mandates that the state reduced credit multiplier vary according to the specific legal entity classification of the taxpayer or partner receiving the credit allocation. Specifically, C corporations multiply their gross California credit by 91.16% (reflecting 100% − 8.84% standard corporate franchise tax rate under R&TC Section 23151), individuals, estates, and trusts multiply their gross credit by 87.70% (reflecting 100% − 12.30% top base individual marginal rate), and S corporations compute their entity-level credit using a 98.50% multiplier (reflecting 100% − 1.50% S-corporation entity-level tax rate under R&TC Section 23802)."
