        "text": "Massachusetts provides a corporate excise research credit under Massachusetts General Laws (M.G.L.) c. 63, § 38M and Department of Revenue regulation 830 CMR 63.38M.1 (Massachusetts Research Credit). Under 830 CMR 63.38M.1(2)–(5), the regulation tethers its core definitions of QREs (§ 41(b)) and basic research payments (§ 41(e)) to the Internal Revenue Code as amended and in effect on August 12, 1991 (while referencing current-year IRC §§ 280C(c) and 704 for net income determination and partnership flow-through attribution under 830 CMR 63.38M.1(3)(d) and (f)), providing a standard credit equal to 10 percent of incremental Massachusetts QREs over the Massachusetts base amount (subject to a 50 percent minimum base floor and a 16 percent fixed-base percentage if historical records are inadequate) plus 15 percent of Massachusetts basic research payments determined under IRC § 41(e)(1)(A). Additionally, following a 2014 statutory amendment to M.G.L. c. 63, § 38M, Massachusetts offers an Alternative Simplified Method (modeled after the federal simplified method and referencing IRC § 41(c)(5) as in effect on January 1, 2014) that phased in from 5 percent (2015–2017) and 7.5 percent (2018–2020) to 10 percent (for calendar years beginning on or after January 1, 2021) of Massachusetts QREs exceeding 50 percent of the average Massachusetts QREs for the three preceding taxable years. Annual utilization is capped at 100 percent of the first $25,000 of corporate excise plus 75 percent of excise over $25,000 (shared across affiliated groups), with a <a href=\"/us/glossary/research-credit-carryforward\">15-year carryforward</a> (or indefinite carryforward in certain instances, and partial refundability available for certified Life Science or Climatetech companies)."
