        "text": "However, the flush language of 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(B) and Treasury Regulation 26 C.F.R. § 1.41-2(d)(2) establish a favorable statutory safe harbor known as the <a href=\"/us/glossary/substantially-all-rule\">\"substantially all\" rule</a> for employee wages. If \"substantially all\" of the services performed by an employee for the taxpayer during the taxable year consist of qualified services meeting the requirements of 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(B)(i) or (ii), then the term \"qualified services\" means all (100%) of the services performed by that employee for the taxpayer during the taxable year. Under 26 C.F.R. § 1.41-2(d)(2), services meeting § 41(b)(2)(B)(i) or (ii) constitute \"substantially all\" of the employee's services only if the wages allocated to those qualified services (on the time-ratio basis used for purposes of § 1.41-2(d)(1)) constitute at least 80% of the total wages paid to or incurred by the taxpayer for the employee during the taxable year."
