        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Fourth, even under the January 1, 2025 IRC conformity update, California explicitly <strong>does not conform</strong> to the federal TCJA (P.L. 115-97, § 13206(a)) <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-174-amortization\">5-year capitalization and amortization rules</a> for IRC § 174 research and experimental expenses. California continues to permit immediate 100% deductibility of R&D expenses (reduced by the amount of the current-year California research credit claimed under R&TC § 24440 unless the taxpayer elects the reduced credit under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-280cc-rd-credit-deduction-disallowance\">IRC § 280C(c)</a>). Unused California research credits <a href=\"/us/glossary/research-credit-carryforward\">carry forward indefinitely</a> until exhausted (R&TC § 23609(f)), subject for taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2024, and before January 1, 2027, to California's <strong>$5,000,000 annual business credit utilization limitation</strong> (with an irrevocable election available on Form FTB 3870 to claim disallowed credits as a 20%-per-year refundable credit over a 5-year period beginning three years after the election)."
