        "text": "The Idaho State Tax Commission disallowed the entire credit by synthesizing three federal precedents governing 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2). First, when the petitioner invoked <em>Suder v. Commissioner</em>, T.C. Memo. 2014-201, to justify oral owner estimates without time-tracking records, the Commission noted that <em>Suder</em> primarily addressed whether CEO compensation was unreasonable as a QRE under former IRC § 174(e) compared to similar-sized companies and did not relieve the petitioner of the requirement to establish a <a href=\"/us/glossary/project-to-expense-nexus\">documented nexus</a> between employees and specific research tasks. Second, quoting the Seventh Circuit's holding in <em>Little Sandy Coal Co., Inc. v. Commissioner</em>, 62 F.4th 287 (7th Cir. 2023), that \"shortcut estimates of experimentation-related activities will not suffice... [s]omething more, such as <a href=\"/us/faq/how-to-track-rd-hours-for-tax-credits\">documentation of time spent on such activities</a>, is necessary,\" the Commission rejected the 60%–65% conversational wage estimates under IRC § 6001, 26 C.F.R. § 1.6001-1(a), and 26 C.F.R. § 1.41-4(d). Third, applying <em>Union Carbide Corp. v. Commissioner</em>, 97 T.C.M. (CCH) 1207 (2009), <em>aff'd</em>, 697 F.3d 104 (2d Cir. 2012), the Commission disallowed the 20% supply estimate (and cutting-floor production wages) because under IRC § 41(d)(2)(C), the truss materials were used to manufacture finished goods sold to customers, were deducted as ordinary cost of goods sold under IRC § 162, and would have been purchased for commercial production regardless of whether any research occurred."
