        "text": "Both the Minnesota Tax Court and the Minnesota Supreme Court rejected that argument and held that Minn. Stat. § 290.068, subd. 2(c) incorporates the 50 percent minimum base amount limitation of I.R.C. § 41(c)(2) in full. First, the court explained that reading Minn. Stat. § 290.068, subd. 2(c) to incorporate only § 41(c)(1) would render \"base amount\" meaningless because § 41(c)(1) depends directly on the companion paragraphs of § 41(c)—including § 41(c)(2) (minimum base amount), § 41(c)(3) (fixed-base percentage), and the consistency and gross receipts rules. Second, turning to legislative history, the court noted that when Minnesota first adopted the federal base amount concept in 1982 (referencing former I.R.C. § 44F(c)), the 50 percent floor was already embedded in § 44F(c)(3); and when Minnesota updated § 290.068 in 1991 to reference \"section 41(c),\" federal § 41(c) contained only provisions defining the boundaries of the base amount, whereas the optional AIC and ASC elections in paragraphs (4) and (5) were added by Congress later in 1996 and 2006."
