          "<strong>Step 2 — Compute Statutory Minimum Base Amount Floor (26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(2)):</strong> Multiplying current-year California QREs of $500,000 by 50.0% yields a minimum base amount floor of $250,000 (0.50 × $500,000 = $250,000).",
          "<strong>Step 3 — Determine Applicable Base Amount:</strong> Selecting the greater of Step 1 ($300,000) or Step 2 ($250,000) establishes an applicable base amount of $300,000. Because the company's current-year QRE-to-gross-receipts ratio (10.0% = $500,000 / $5,000,000) does not exceed twice its fixed-base percentage (2 × 6.00% = 12.00%), the 50 percent minimum base amount floor of § 41(c)(2) is not triggered.",
          "<strong>Step 4 — Compute Excess QREs and Gross California Regular Research Credit:</strong> Subtracting the $300,000 applicable base amount from $500,000 of current-year QREs leaves $200,000 in excess QREs ($500,000 − $300,000 = $200,000), which multiplied by the 15.0% California RRC rate yields a total California Regular Research Credit of $30,000 ($200,000 × 15.0% = $30,000), representing an effective credit yield of 6.00% ($30,000 / $500,000) on total current-year California QREs before any § 280C(c) reduction."
