          "<strong>Step 2 — Compute Statutory Minimum Base Amount Floor (26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(2) & LDG 2012-03-01):</strong> Multiplying current-year California QREs of $2,000,000 by 50.0% yields a minimum base amount floor of $1,000,000 (0.50 × $2,000,000 = $1,000,000).",
          "<strong>Step 3 — Determine Applicable Base Amount:</strong> Selecting the greater of Step 1 ($0) or Step 2 ($1,000,000) establishes an applicable base amount of $1,000,000, as FTB LDG 2012-03-01 and 26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(2) override the $0 calculated base amount and impose the mandatory 50 percent floor.",
          "<strong>Step 4 — Compute Excess QREs and Gross California Regular Research Credit:</strong> Subtracting the $1,000,000 applicable base amount from $2,000,000 of current-year QREs leaves $1,000,000 in excess QREs ($2,000,000 − $1,000,000 = $1,000,000), which multiplied by the 15.0% California RRC rate yields a total California Regular Research Credit of $150,000 ($1,000,000 × 15.0% = $150,000). Without the 26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(2) floor, a $0 base amount would have produced a $300,000 credit ($2,000,000 × 15.0%); the 50 percent floor neutralizes $1,000,000 of QREs and halves the gross credit to $150,000 (7.50% of total current-year QREs). By comparison, if SaaS Corp instead elected the new 2025 California ASC (assuming prior three-year average California QREs of $1,500,000, so the 50% historical QRE base is $750,000), its ASC would be $37,500 (($2,000,000 − $750,000) × 3.0%), or $26,000 ($2,000,000 × 1.3%) if it lacked QREs in any of the prior three years—demonstrating why a $0 gross-receipts taxpayer obtains a much larger credit ($150,000) by remaining on the Regular Research Credit despite the 50 percent floor."
