        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "California operates under a selective, fixed-date conformity regime. SB 711 updated the state's general IRC conformity date to January 1, 2025, a date that precedes the July 4, 2025 enactment of Public Law 119-21, so OBBBA's changes are not part of California law. California never adopted the TCJA amendments to Section 174, and SB 711 continued that nonconformity: California does not adopt the federal capitalization and amortization rules for research and experimental expenditures under either Section 174 or Section 174A, and both domestic and foreign research expenditures remain deductible for California purposes under the pre-TCJA rules. In practice, a corporation therefore deducts its domestic research expenditures currently on both its federal return (under Section 174A) and its California return (under California's pre-TCJA Section 174 conformity), while foreign research expenditures are amortized over 15 years federally but deducted for California purposes. Taxpayers that <a href=\"/us/glossary/r-and-e-capitalization\">capitalized research expenditures</a> federally in 2022 through 2024 should already have reported a federal-state difference for California for those years. Other states analyze Section 41 claims with reference to the same federal definitions; for example, states that adopt IRC § 41(b) apply the federal rule that property subject to depreciation is not a supply QRE."
