        "text": "Applying the ASC rates for the 2025 tax year under IRC § 41(c)(4) and R&TC § 23609(h)(2)(A) (elected for California on Form FTB 3523) shows the arithmetic effect of these rules. In the first scenario, assume AeroTech's average annual federal QREs for 2022 through 2024 were $600,000 and its average annual California QREs for the same period were $400,000. The federal ASC base equals 50 percent of $600,000, or $300,000, yielding federal excess QREs of $865,000 − $300,000 = $565,000 and a 14 percent federal ASC of $565,000 × 0.14 = $79,100. If AeroTech makes the <a href=\"/us/glossary/reduced-research-credit\">reduced credit election</a> under IRC § 280C(c)(2), the federal credit becomes $79,100 × 0.79 = $62,489, and AeroTech is not required to reduce its Section 174A deduction by the credit; without the election, the full $79,100 credit is claimed and the Section 174A expenditures are reduced by $79,100. On the California return in the same scenario, the California ASC base equals 50 percent of $400,000, or $200,000, yielding California excess QREs of $565,000 − $200,000 = $365,000 and a 3 percent California ASC of $365,000 × 0.03 = $10,950."
