      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Technical Substantiation and Audit Defense Implications"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under the standards applied in <em>Union Carbide</em>, <em>Leon Max</em>, <em>Little Sandy Coal</em>, <em>Swat-Fame</em>, <em>First Solar</em>, and <em>Electronic Data Systems</em>, <a href=\"/us/glossary/credible-oral-testimony\">after-the-fact oral assertions</a>, surveys prepared after the research ended, audited financial statement line items, and generalized cost-center allocations are not enough on their own to prove that activities satisfy 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1)."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Instead, substantiating that an activity satisfies 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1) calls for <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">technical records created during the research</a>, such as statements of the technical uncertainty, the hypotheses or design alternatives considered, simulation and trial logs, failure analyses, code-versioning histories, and engineering diagrams. Those records should be paired with <a href=\"/us/faq/how-to-track-rd-hours-for-tax-credits\">time-tracking</a> detailed enough to compute the 80 percent \"substantially all\" fraction under Treasury Regulation § 1.41-4(a)(6) for each business component (or for a properly defined subset under the shrinking-back rule of Treasury Regulation § 1.41-4(b)(2)), to tie the qualifying activities to the claimed wage, supply, computer-use, and contract research amounts, and to establish in-state performance under state statutes such as California R&TC § 23609(c)(2). The federal <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-6765\">Form 6765</a> requirements that the IRS has phased in for business-component-level information make the same point from the reporting side: the credit is claimed and defended one business component at a time."
      }
