        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "26 U.S. Code Section 41(e) serves as a specialized fiscal mechanism within the Internal Revenue Code, offering eligible Subchapter C corporations a 20% federal tax credit on 100% of incremental cash payments made to qualified educational and scientific research organizations for foundational research above the Qualified Organization Base Period Amount. When paired with state R&D tax credit statutes—such as California Revenue and Taxation Code Section 23609, which elevates the basic research credit rate to 24%, expands substantive eligibility to applied commercial product research conducted physically within California, and permits immediate R&E deductibility despite federal capitalization rules, or Massachusetts General Laws Chapter 63, Section 38M, which provides a 15% basic research credit alongside refundable MLSC life sciences incentives—corporate-university partnerships can generate substantial multijurisdictional tax offsets."
